## 1. The Big Idea

_One sentence: **Cognitive enhancement is not a ladder you climb to better drugs — it's a power-supply schematic where one base rail stays lit year-round (Level 1, where you live), a gate stops most signals from going higher, and only the few that pass get routed — by exact bottleneck — to a single matching lever, with the brain's drugs sitting at the top as a guarded, momentary override.** The poster should make the reader feel, instantly, that the base is the whole system and the "levels" above it are rare, gated detours — the inverse of the "level-up" instinct they walked in with._

## 2. Visual Metaphor & Structure — the hero object

**A vertical "neural power-supply" schematic of the brain, read bottom-to-top, dominated by an always-on base rail.** This is the anchor; nothing replaces it. It is deliberately *not* a staircase.

- **The base rail (bottom ~45% of the hero — it must dominate by area):** a wide, full-width, **steadily-glowing bus** labeled `LEVEL 1 — THE CONSTANT BASE · run year-round`, sub-captioned `the realistic ceiling for almost everyone`. Draw it as a live circuit rail with small steady indicator LEDs and etched feed-lines coming in from below (`SLEEP · FOOD · BEHAVIOUR · STRUCTURAL STACK`). **This rail never breaks and never switches off** — it runs unbroken all the way up the left edge of the poster, *behind* every level above it. That continuous lit rail **is** the one rule made visible: the overlays tap *into* it; they never replace it.

- **The gate (a horizontal checkpoint sitting on top of the base, ~12%):** a labeled barrier `THE "DO YOU EVEN NEED THIS?" GATE` with four pass-conditions rendered as four small toggles that must *all* read green to let signal through: `BASE MAXED · SLEEP FIXED · MEASURED CEILING · REWARD > RISK`. Most signal arriving from Level 1 visibly **stops here** (show a few traces dead-ending at the gate, dimmed). Only one trace passes upward. This is the emotional reversal: the path up is mostly closed.

- **Level 2 — the diagnostic router (~28%, the busiest band):** the single passing signal enters a **four-way junction / switchboard** labeled `LEVEL 2 — THE HEAVY DAY · diagnose, then add ONE`. Four clearly separated output channels, each a different bottleneck with its matching lever as a single inline switch:
  - `CAN'T START` → *drive / dopamine* → `tyrosine + caffeine`
  - `CAN'T FOCUS` → *calm-focus* → `2:1 theanine : caffeine ↓`
  - `CAN'T DECIDE` → *too much signal* → `GABA / serotonin · detach`
  - `CAN'T LEARN` → *acetylcholine* → `choline pathway`
  Exactly **one** channel is shown energised (lit cyan); the other three are dark. A small etched rule sits across the junction: `ROUTE — DON'T DUMP. add one lever, measure, keep or drop.`

- **Level 3 — the guarded override (top ~15%, small and scarce):** a **momentary, spring-return switch behind a flip-up safety guard**, labeled `LEVEL 3 — THE URGENT OVERRIDE · situational, rare`. One worked case etched beside it: `BLOWN-SLEEP DEADLINE → modafinil (drop the caffeine)`. A `↺ RESET RULE` micro-warning: `low · fixed · cue-bound. a swing weapon, not a daily driver.` It is physically the smallest element on the poster — scarcity is the point.

- **The fault detail (the racetam-headache proof, a small inset off to one side):** one channel drawn wired to a **dead/dark stretch of base rail** throws a red fault indicator: `OVERLAY ON A BARE BASE → ⚠ racetam headache`. It silently proves the one rule — *the lever only works on a fuelled pathway.* Keep it small; it rewards the second look.

**Why this metaphor fits and nothing else does.** The article's literal claims are "base," "rail it runs on," "levels," "lever," "switch," "gate," "route the signal," "swing weapon." A power-supply / signal-routing schematic is the *only* shape that is simultaneously (a) honest to that language, (b) able to show the **base dominating by area** (you live on Level 1), (c) able to show the climb as **gated and rare** rather than aspirational, and (d) able to keep the base rail **lit and unbroken behind every level** — which is the one rule. A staircase, a pyramid, or a "level-up" meter would all encode the exact misconception (linear ascent to a better top) the article is written to destroy.

## 3. Layout Description

**Portrait (poster / blog-hero, ~4:5 or 3:4)** so the vertical climb has room and the base rail can sit massive at the bottom. (A landscape variant works for an in-article banner; portrait is primary.)

- **Top band (~10%):** the headline (see §4), small, kerned wide on the dark ground, with a thin rule under it. The guarded Level-3 switch peeks just below it — the top of the system is the *smallest, most locked* part.
- **Center stage (~78%) — the hero:** the schematic, read **bottom-to-top against the climb instinct**. The eye is meant to *start at the bottom* (the huge glowing base) and feel that this is home, then travel **up** through the gate (where most traces die), into the four-way router (find the one lit channel), and arrive at the tiny guarded override at the top. The continuous lit base rail runs up the left margin the entire height, tying every level back to Level 1.
- **Right margin micro-legend (thin, vertical):** the spine question set as a vertical lockup — `WHAT'S THE SMALLEST INPUT THAT FIXES TODAY?` — read bottom-up alongside the climb.
- **Bottom footer (~12%, under the base):** the thesis lockup `ENHANCE THE PATHWAY. DON'T REPLACE THE BASE.`, plus a thin footer line: `Part 4.0 of 4 · Cognitive Enhancement · the Productive pillar · the framework before the stacks`.

Reading path is deliberate and *inverts* the climb: **rest on the base → most signal stops at the gate → the one that passes is routed to a single matching lever → the rare, guarded override at the very top → back down to the rule that the base was lit the whole time.**

