## 5. Color & Mood

**Mood:** the calm, lit-up seriousness of a control room or a clean schematic at night — disciplined, anti-hype, quietly authoritative. Not motivational, not "biohacker neon." The feeling of being shown the wiring of a decision you thought was a free-for-all. Clinical precision rendered as live circuitry.

**Palette (dark-first, within `aether-call-to-action-DESIGN.md` tokens):**
- **Background:** near-black ink (`#0B0F14`-ish), a barely-there top-to-bottom gradient (slightly cooler/darker at the top where the system locks down, faintly warmer glow at the base where the rail lives).
- **Live signal — the base rail + the one routed channel:** **electric teal / cyan** (`#2DD4BF`-ish). Cyan = "powered, live, healthy signal." The base glows steady cyan; the single energised Level-2 channel glows the same cyan — visually linking *the overlay to the base it runs on.*
- **The gate:** a muted **amber / caution** (`#E8A23D`-ish) for the checkpoint and its toggles — "stop and be honest here."
- **One alarm color — used only twice:** restrained **signal red / coral** (`#FF5C5C`), reserved *exclusively* for (a) the racetam-headache fault inset and (b) the Level-3 `RESET RULE` warning. Scarcity is what gives it force.
- **Structure / text / ruler:** soft off-white (`#E6E8EE`) for primary labels, muted slate for secondary — never harsh pure white.
- **Dimming = inactive:** dead-ended traces at the gate and the three un-chosen router channels are drawn in **dim grey**, not colored. Only the live path is cyan. The eye instantly reads *one lit path through a mostly-dark system* — which is the whole thesis.

The single-lit-path-through-a-dark-board does double duty: it's striking *and* it encodes "diagnose, don't dump" before a word is read — most of the board is off on purpose.

## 6. Style Reference — **LOCKS the Productive / Cognitive Enhancement pillar style**

**"Neural Signal Schematic" — a circuit/power-supply diagram of the mind, rendered with restraint.** This is the locked style for every Cognitive Enhancement poster (Parts 1.0 → 4.3). Touchstones:

- The honesty and labeled-trace discipline of a **real electronic schematic / signal-flow diagram** (junctions, rails, gated switches, indicator LEDs) — credible, not decorative.
- The dark, layered elegance of **editorial science graphics** (*Quanta* / *Scientific American* full-page diagrams; a Wait-But-Why concept rendered for grown-ups, at the console).
- A touch of **control-panel / power-supply** language: an always-on base bus, momentary guarded switches, a checkpoint gate.

It sits as a deliberate **sibling to the Fit pillar's "Engineering Diagnostic Instrument"** — same instrument-grade labeling (JetBrains Mono), same single-alarm-color discipline, same ruler-drawn precision — but where **Fit instruments a body you read on a workbench**, **Productive wires a mind you route signal through.** Conventions to carry across the pillar: dark ground; cyan = live signal / base; amber = caution/gate; one scarce red for faults & reset warnings; dim grey for inactive paths; the brain framed as a *routing system with an always-on base rail*, never a glowing organ.

## 7. What to Avoid

- **The "smart-drug" cliché kit — a glowing brain, a lightbulb head, gears turning in a skull, neurons firing as fireworks, or pills/capsules floating around a head.** It screams "nootropics" and teaches nothing; it also makes the *drugs* the hero when the article's entire point is that the **base** is the hero and the drugs are a tiny guarded switch.
- **A symmetric ascending staircase, a "level-up" game meter, or a tidy 3-step podium that implies enhancement is a linear progression you climb.** That is the exact misconception the article exists to kill. The base must **dominate by area**, the climb must read **gated and rare** (most traces die at the gate), and the base rail must stay **lit and unbroken behind every level**. If the graphic could be redrawn as an aspirational ascending ladder without losing information, it has failed the article.
- (Bonus to avoid: lighting up all four router channels at once — it would quietly endorse "dump everything," the precise error of "diagnose, don't dump." Exactly **one** channel may be live.)
